select 'bbbb0003-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Strategy is best described as...","options":["a detailed annual budget","an integrated set of choices about where to compete and how to win","a mission statement on the wall","the list of everything the company could possibly do"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strategy is a small set of hard, connected choices - a budget or a slogan is not a strategy on its own. See ''What strategy is (and isn''t)''."},{"prompt":"In Porter''s Five Forces, powerful suppliers tend to...","options":["raise the profitability of the industry","squeeze the profits of the firms that buy from them","have no effect on rivalry","always be a threat of new entry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When suppliers have leverage they capture more of the value, leaving less for the industry. See ''Porter''s Five Forces''."},{"prompt":"In the VRIO framework, a resource gives a lasting edge only if it is valuable, rare, costly to imitate, and...","options":["organized so the firm can exploit it","owned by the founder","protected by a patent","cheap to produce"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The O in VRIO is Organization - the firm must actually be set up to capture the value. See ''Resources, capabilities, and VRIO''."},{"prompt":"A cost-leadership strategy aims to...","options":["charge the highest price in the market","be the lowest-cost producer while offering an acceptable product","serve only a tiny niche","avoid competing on price entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cost leaders win by driving costs below rivals while keeping quality acceptable, not by being fanciest. See ''Cost leadership vs differentiation''."},{"prompt":"Vertical integration means a company...","options":["enters a completely unrelated industry","takes ownership of steps up or down its own supply chain","merges with a direct competitor","sells shares to the public"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vertical integration is moving upstream (toward suppliers) or downstream (toward customers) in your value chain. See ''Corporate strategy''."},{"prompt":"A SWOT analysis pairs internal strengths and weaknesses with external...","options":["revenues and costs","opportunities and threats","assets and liabilities","products and prices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SWOT joins the internal S and W with the external O and T. See ''SWOT synthesis''."},{"prompt":"The Business Model Canvas describes how a firm creates, delivers, and captures...","options":["taxes","value","inventory","headcount"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A business model is the logic of how an organization creates, delivers, and captures value. See ''Business models and the Canvas''."},{"prompt":"The balanced scorecard was designed to keep managers from...","options":["hiring too many people","judging performance on financial numbers alone","writing a mission statement","entering foreign markets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It balances financial measures with customer, process, and learning measures. See ''Execution: KPIs and the balanced scorecard''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What strategy is (and isn''t)', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell a real strategy apart from a goal, a slogan, or a budget, and name the two questions every strategy must answer. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, why is ''we will grow 20 percent this year'' a goal rather than a strategy?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A strategy is an integrated set of choices about where a firm will compete and how it will win there. ''Where'' means the arena: which customers, which products, which geographies, which stage of the value chain. ''How'' means the advantage: the reason a chosen customer picks you over the alternatives and keeps picking you. Two features make choices strategic. First, they are integrated - the pieces reinforce each other rather than pulling in different directions. Second, they involve real trade-offs - saying yes to one arena means saying no to others. A plan with no sacrifice is usually just a wish list. Strategy is not the same as a goal (the target), a mission (the purpose), operational effectiveness (running the same activities better), or a budget (the money). Those matter, but none of them tells you how you will beat a specific rival for a specific customer."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"Southwest Airlines built an integrated strategy around one arena and one advantage: short, point-to-point flights on a single aircraft type, no assigned seats, no meals, fast gate turnarounds. Every choice reinforces low cost and quick turns, and each one is a deliberate no to the full-service model. IKEA chose flat-pack, self-assembly furniture sold in large out-of-town stores; the customer does the delivery and assembly, which lets IKEA hold prices down - again, a coherent web of choices, not a single clever trick. Compare that with a company that says ''we aim to be the leading provider of quality solutions.'' That sentence names no arena, no trade-off, and no reason a customer would switch. It is a platitude, not a strategy."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Turn this platitude into a real strategic claim: ''we want to delight customers.'' 2) Name the two questions every strategy must answer. 3) Is ''cut costs 5 percent across every department'' a strategy or an operational goal? 4) Why does a genuine strategy require saying no to something? 5) Southwest flies only one type of aircraft - name one cost this single choice reduces. Answers: 1) Example: ''we will win price-sensitive commuters on busy short-haul routes by turning planes around in 25 minutes and flying a single aircraft type.'' 2) Where will we compete, and how will we win there? 3) An operational goal - it improves efficiency but names no arena or advantage. 4) Because resources and positioning are finite; serving everyone the same way lets a focused rival beat you on the customers you both want. 5) Training, spare parts, and maintenance are all simpler with one aircraft type, lowering cost."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling a goal or a mission statement a strategy - the target and the purpose are not the same as the plan to win. Mistake: confusing operational effectiveness (doing the same activities better) with strategy (doing different activities, or the same ones in a different configuration). Exit: (a) What two questions must a strategy answer? (b) What is the difference between a strategy and a budget? Answers: where to compete and how to win; a budget allocates money, while a strategy chooses the arena and the advantage that the money is meant to serve."}]}'::jsonb, 'A useful test: if a statement could be pasted into a rival''s plan without anyone noticing, it is not yet a strategy - it is a platitude.', 12),
  (3, 'Mission, vision, and values', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish mission, vision, and values, and judge whether each one is specific enough to guide a real decision. Warm-up: which of the three describes a future state the company is reaching toward?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A mission states why the organization exists and whom it serves - its purpose in the present. A vision describes the future the organization is trying to bring about, a destination that is ambitious but not yet reached. Values are the small set of behaviors the organization commits to living by on the way. Together they set the guardrails for strategy: strategy chooses where to compete and how to win, and mission, vision, and values keep those choices pointed at a shared purpose. The test of a good statement is not how inspiring it sounds but whether it can settle an argument. If two managers disagree about a decision and the mission tells them which way to go, it is doing its job. If any company in any industry could adopt the sentence unchanged, it is decoration."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A mission like ''to organize the world''s information and make it universally accessible and useful'' names a clear job and a clear beneficiary - you can test a project against it. Patagonia''s stated purpose of building the best product while causing no unnecessary environmental harm actually shapes decisions about materials and repair programs, which is what makes it more than a slogan. A vision points further out: an ambition to see a future the firm is working to create, such as sustainable transport becoming the norm. Contrast these with ''to be a world-class organization delivering excellence to stakeholders,'' which names no customer, no purpose, and no behavior, and so guides nothing."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which statement describes the present purpose: mission or vision? 2) Rewrite this weak value into a testable behavior: ''we value integrity.'' 3) A team is deciding whether to launch a cheaper, lower-quality product line. Which of the three - mission, vision, or values - should they consult first? 4) Give the one-line test that separates a real mission from a platitude. 5) True or false: a vision should already be achieved. Answers: 1) Mission. 2) Example: ''we tell customers about a defect before they discover it, even when it costs us the sale.'' 3) The mission and values - does the cheaper line still serve the stated purpose and behaviors. 4) Could a rival in another industry use this exact sentence; if yes, it is a platitude. 5) False - a vision is a future state still being reached toward."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: swapping mission and vision - purpose now versus destination later. Mistake: writing values so broad (''excellence'', ''integrity'') that they never rule anything out. Exit: (a) What does a vision describe? (b) What is the practical test of whether a mission is useful? Answers: the future state the organization is trying to create; whether it can settle a real disagreement about what to do."}]}'::jsonb, 'Mission answers ''why do we exist now''; vision answers ''what do we want to become''; values answer ''how will we behave getting there''. Keep the three straight.', 11),
  (4, 'External analysis: PESTEL', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Use the six PESTEL lenses to scan the macro environment for forces that could reshape an industry, and sort a raw observation into the right category. Warm-up: is a new data-privacy law a Political, Legal, or Social force - or could it be more than one?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"PESTEL is a checklist for the broad forces surrounding every business, the ones a single firm cannot control but must anticipate. Political covers government stability, trade policy, and taxation. Economic covers growth, interest rates, inflation, and exchange rates. Social covers demographics, lifestyles, and shifting attitudes. Technological covers new methods and tools that change what is possible or cheap. Environmental covers climate, resource scarcity, and sustainability pressure. Legal covers laws and regulation the firm must obey. The point is not to fill six boxes for their own sake but to spot the two or three forces that could genuinely change the rules of the game, then ask: is this an opportunity or a threat, how soon, and how big?"},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"For a global automaker, the Environmental and Legal lenses dominate: tightening emissions rules and consumer pressure push the whole industry toward electric drivetrains, turning a compliance cost into a race for advantage. For a streaming service, the Technological lens (faster home broadband) and the Social lens (viewers cutting traditional cable) opened the arena in the first place, while the Economic lens (household budgets tightening) later pushed firms toward ad-supported tiers. For a fast-fashion retailer, Social attitudes toward sustainability and Legal rules on supply-chain disclosure are moving in the same direction at once, which is exactly the kind of reinforcing signal a scan is meant to catch."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the letter T stand for in PESTEL? 2) Rising interest rates that raise a firm''s borrowing cost fall under which lens? 3) An aging population that shifts demand toward healthcare - which lens? 4) A carbon tax could sit in two lenses at once; name them. 5) Give the two questions to ask about any force you identify. Answers: 1) Technological. 2) Economic. 3) Social (demographics). 4) Environmental and Legal (and arguably Political). 5) Is it an opportunity or a threat, and how soon and how big is its likely impact?"},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: listing every fact you can think of instead of isolating the few forces that could change the industry''s rules. Mistake: mixing up macro forces (PESTEL) with industry rivalry (Five Forces) - a new competitor is a Five Forces issue, not a PESTEL one. Exit: (a) Name the six PESTEL lenses. (b) What single question turns a listed force into a strategic insight? Answers: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental, Legal; whether it is an opportunity or a threat, and how soon and how large."}]}'::jsonb, 'PESTEL scans the macro environment - the wide forces no single firm controls. Save industry rivals for Five Forces; PESTEL is the weather, not the other boats.', 12),
  (5, 'External analysis: Porter''s Five Forces', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Use Porter''s five forces to judge how attractive an industry is and explain why some industries are chronically more profitable than others. Warm-up: name an industry where it is very easy for a new competitor to enter - what does that do to prices?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Porter''s Five Forces model explains an industry''s underlying profitability through five pressures. Threat of new entrants: if outsiders can enter easily, they compete prices and profits down, so high barriers (heavy capital, strong brands, network effects) protect incumbents. Bargaining power of suppliers: concentrated or unique suppliers capture more of the value. Bargaining power of buyers: large or well-informed customers push prices down. Threat of substitutes: a different product that meets the same need caps how much anyone can charge. Rivalry among existing competitors: intense price and feature wars erode margins. When most forces are strong, the industry is a hard place to earn money; when most are weak, it is comfortable. The forces are also a map of moves - a firm can try to raise entry barriers, reduce buyer power, or blunt substitutes."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"Commercial aviation is a textbook tough industry: buyers compare fares in seconds, fuel suppliers hold power, high-speed rail and video calls act as substitutes, and rivalry on price is relentless - which is why margins are thin despite full planes. Compare a company like a dominant search or desktop-software provider whose network effects and switching costs raise entry barriers, weaken buyer power, and leave rivalry muted, producing famously high margins. In grocery retail, buyer power is high and rivalry fierce, so chains fight on scale and cost. Reading the five forces first tells you how much profit the industry itself allows before any single firm''s cleverness is even considered."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the five forces. 2) A pharmaceutical patent that blocks copycats affects which force most directly? 3) If buyers can easily switch between sellers, is their bargaining power high or low? 4) Video calls replacing some business flights are an example of which force? 5) Why do high barriers to entry tend to keep an industry''s profits higher? Answers: 1) Threat of new entrants, supplier power, buyer power, threat of substitutes, and rivalry among existing competitors. 2) Threat of new entrants - the patent is a barrier that keeps copycats out. 3) High. 4) Threat of substitutes. 5) Fewer new rivals arrive to compete prices down, so incumbents keep more of the value."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating Five Forces as a scorecard of one company rather than an x-ray of the whole industry''s structure. Mistake: confusing a substitute (a different product meeting the same need, like rail versus flying) with a direct rival (another airline). Exit: (a) Which force does a strong brand and heavy capital requirement most affect? (b) When most forces are strong, is the industry easy or hard to profit in? Answers: the threat of new entrants (it raises barriers to entry); hard."}]}'::jsonb, 'Five Forces explains why an industry is profitable or not - it describes the structure, not any one company. Strong forces squeeze profit; weak forces leave more on the table.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The clearest sign that a statement is a real strategy rather than a platitude is that it...","options":["sounds inspiring","involves a genuine trade-off","mentions the word quality","sets a revenue target"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strategy requires saying no to something; a claim with no trade-off is a wish, not a plan."},{"prompt":"Which statement is a vision rather than a mission?","options":["We exist to move packages safely for small businesses today","We serve commuters on busy short routes","We are working toward a world where clean transport is the default","We repair before we replace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A vision describes a future state the firm is reaching toward, not its present purpose."},{"prompt":"A sudden rise in inflation belongs to which PESTEL lens?","options":["Political","Economic","Social","Legal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inflation, growth, and interest rates are Economic forces."},{"prompt":"A new consumer data-protection statute belongs most directly to which PESTEL lens?","options":["Technological","Environmental","Legal","Economic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A statute the firm must obey is a Legal force (with a Political flavor)."},{"prompt":"In Porter''s model, easy entry into an industry tends to...","options":["raise industry profits","lower industry profits","eliminate substitutes","reduce buyer power"],"answer":1,"explanation":"New entrants compete prices and margins down, lowering profitability."},{"prompt":"High-speed rail competing with short flights is an example of...","options":["a new entrant","supplier power","a substitute","buyer power"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rail is a different product meeting the same need - a substitute that caps prices."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Internal analysis: resources, capabilities, and VRIO', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Separate resources from capabilities and run any candidate through the four VRIO questions to judge whether it can support a lasting advantage. Warm-up: is a strong brand a resource or a capability?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"External analysis tells you what the world allows; internal analysis tells you what you can bring. Resources are the assets a firm holds - tangible ones like factories and cash, and intangible ones like brands, patents, and data. Capabilities are what a firm can do reliably by combining resources - designing beloved products, running a fast supply chain, delighting customers at scale. The resource-based view argues that durable advantage comes from resources and capabilities rivals cannot easily copy. VRIO turns that into four questions. Valuable: does it help exploit an opportunity or blunt a threat? Rare: do few rivals have it? Costly to imitate: is it hard or expensive to copy or substitute? Organized to capture value: is the firm actually structured to use it? A resource that is valuable and rare but easily copied gives only a temporary edge; only when all four hold do you get a sustained advantage."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A coffee chain''s roasting equipment is valuable but not rare, so it yields no edge by itself. Its store-level service culture, built over decades, is valuable, rare, and genuinely hard to imitate because it lives in thousands of trained people - and because the company is organized to hire and reward for it, that culture becomes a sustained advantage. A logistics giant''s dense delivery network is costly to imitate because a rival would need years and enormous capital to match the coverage. By contrast, a single popular product feature is often valuable and briefly rare but cheap to copy, which is why it delivers only a temporary lead until competitors catch up."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Classify each as resource or capability: a patent; the ability to launch a new phone every year on time. 2) What does the R in VRIO stand for? 3) A resource is valuable and rare but easy to copy - temporary or sustained advantage? 4) What does the O in VRIO add that the first three miss? 5) Why does a proprietary dataset that competitors cannot legally obtain score well on ''costly to imitate''? Answers: 1) A patent is a resource; the reliable on-time annual launch is a capability. 2) Rare. 3) Temporary. 4) Organization - whether the firm is actually set up to exploit the resource, so value is captured rather than wasted. 5) Rivals cannot simply buy or rebuild it, so imitation is blocked or very costly."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating any strength as a source of advantage - if it is not rare and hard to copy, rivals have it too. Mistake: forgetting Organization, so a firm owns a great resource but is not structured to use it. Exit: (a) Name the four VRIO tests. (b) What separates a temporary edge from a sustained one in VRIO terms? Answers: Valuable, Rare, costly to Imitate, Organized to capture value; a sustained advantage requires all four, especially costly-to-imitate plus organization."}]}'::jsonb, 'A resource is something you have; a capability is something you can do repeatedly. VRIO then asks whether that thing can actually carry an advantage.', 13),
  (8, 'SWOT synthesis', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Combine external and internal analysis into a SWOT and, more importantly, turn the four boxes into actual strategic moves. Warm-up: which two SWOT letters come from the outside world?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"SWOT is the bridge between the external scan (PESTEL and Five Forces) and the internal scan (resources, capabilities, VRIO). Strengths and Weaknesses are internal - what you do well or badly. Opportunities and Threats are external - what the environment offers or endangers. On its own a SWOT is just four lists, and a pile of lists is not a decision. The value comes from crossing the boxes to generate moves: use a Strength to seize an Opportunity (attack), use a Strength to defend against a Threat (defend), fix a Weakness that would block an Opportunity (build), and watch where a Weakness meets a Threat (danger). Good SWOT items are specific and, ideally, backed by the earlier frameworks - a strength should be something VRIO would actually rate, not a comforting adjective."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A carmaker with deep battery expertise (Strength, and VRIO-worthy) facing tightening emissions rules (a Threat that is also an Opportunity) can cross the two into a clear move: lead the shift to electric models before regulation forces slower rivals to scramble. A retailer whose Weakness is a thin online presence, meeting the Threat of a fast-shipping online giant, sits squarely in the danger quadrant - the honest read is that the weakness and the threat reinforce each other and demand urgent investment. A streaming firm''s Strength in original content lets it seize the Opportunity of cord-cutting households, an attack move that turns an external trend into growth."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which two SWOT categories are internal? 2) A new low-cost competitor entering your market - Weakness or Threat? 3) A strong balance sheet during a downturn when rivals are cash-starved - Strength or Opportunity, and how might you cross them? 4) Why is a SWOT of four lists not yet a strategy? 5) A ''strength'' that every rival also has - is it truly a strength in strategic terms? Answers: 1) Strengths and Weaknesses. 2) A Threat (it is external). 3) The cash is a Strength; the weakened rivals are an Opportunity - cross them by acquiring assets or share cheaply. 4) Because it lists conditions but makes no choice; the moves come from crossing the boxes. 5) No - if it is not rare it fails VRIO and gives no advantage."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: filing external forces (a rival, a new law) as internal Weaknesses. Mistake: stopping at four lists instead of crossing them into attack, defend, build, and danger moves. Exit: (a) Which two categories are external? (b) What is the real output of a good SWOT? Answers: Opportunities and Threats; specific strategic moves generated by crossing internal strengths and weaknesses against external opportunities and threats."}]}'::jsonb, 'Strengths and weaknesses are internal (about you); opportunities and threats are external (about the world). Do not file a rival''s move under Weakness - it is a Threat.', 11),
  (9, 'Competitive advantage: cost leadership vs differentiation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the two generic routes to competitive advantage, why a focus strategy narrows either one to a niche, and the risk of being caught in the middle. Warm-up: can a company usually be both the cheapest and the most premium at the same time?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Competitive advantage means creating more value than rivals for a chosen set of customers, in a way that is hard to copy. Porter argued there are two basic ways to do it. Cost leadership: become the lowest-cost producer through scale, efficient processes, and tight overhead, then either underprice rivals or match their price and pocket a bigger margin. Differentiation: offer something buyers value enough to pay a premium for - superior design, brand, reliability, service, or ecosystem. A focus strategy applies either route to a narrow segment, serving a niche better than broad players can. The danger is being ''stuck in the middle'': neither the lowest cost nor meaningfully different, so you lose cost-sensitive buyers to the cost leader and premium buyers to the differentiator. The choice must fit the firm''s VRIO resources - you pursue the advantage your capabilities can actually sustain."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A warehouse retailer competes on cost leadership: limited selection, bulk sizes, lean stores, and huge buying power drive costs down, and the savings pull in members. A premium smartphone maker competes on differentiation: tight hardware-software integration, brand, and an ecosystem let it command higher prices even though cheaper phones exist. A boutique running-shoe brand serving serious marathoners is a focus-differentiation play - narrow segment, willing to pay for specialized performance. The cautionary case is a mid-market department store that is neither the cheapest nor clearly special; discounters undercut it and premium brands out-differentiate it, and it slowly loses both ends."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the two generic strategies. 2) A firm sells at the same price as rivals but produces far more cheaply - which advantage is it pursuing? 3) What is a focus strategy? 4) Describe the ''stuck in the middle'' problem in one sentence. 5) Why must the chosen strategy fit the firm''s VRIO resources? Answers: 1) Cost leadership and differentiation. 2) Cost leadership - lower cost at an equal price means a bigger margin. 3) Applying cost leadership or differentiation to a narrow niche served better than broad rivals can. 4) Being neither cheapest nor clearly different, so you lose value-seekers to the cost leader and premium buyers to the differentiator. 5) An advantage lasts only if resources make it valuable, rare, and costly to imitate; choosing a route your capabilities cannot sustain invites imitation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: equating cost leadership with lowest price - it is lowest cost, which can be turned into margin instead of a price cut. Mistake: trying to be both broad cost leader and broad differentiator at once and ending up stuck in the middle. Exit: (a) What are the two generic strategies? (b) What happens to a firm stuck in the middle? Answers: cost leadership and differentiation; it loses cost-sensitive customers to the cost leader and premium customers to the differentiator."}]}'::jsonb, 'Cost leadership means being the low-cost producer, not necessarily the low-price seller. A cost leader can charge an average price and simply keep the fatter margin.', 13),
  (10, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In VRIO, a resource that is valuable and rare but easy to copy yields...","options":["a sustained competitive advantage","a temporary competitive advantage","no advantage at all","a permanent monopoly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without costly-to-imitate, rivals catch up, so the edge is only temporary."},{"prompt":"The O in VRIO stands for...","options":["Ownership","Organization","Opportunity","Output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Organization asks whether the firm is actually set up to exploit the resource."},{"prompt":"A competitor launching a rival product should appear in a SWOT under...","options":["Strengths","Weaknesses","Threats","Opportunities"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A rival''s move is external, so it is a Threat, not an internal Weakness."},{"prompt":"The main weakness of a plain SWOT is that...","options":["it ignores internal factors","four lists make no decision on their own","it only uses financial data","it cannot include threats"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Value comes from crossing the boxes into moves, not from the lists themselves."},{"prompt":"A firm that is the lowest-cost producer but charges the same price as rivals is pursuing...","options":["differentiation","cost leadership","a focus niche","being stuck in the middle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lower cost at an equal price is cost leadership captured as extra margin."},{"prompt":"Being ''stuck in the middle'' means a firm is...","options":["the market leader","neither lowest cost nor clearly differentiated","focused on a niche","vertically integrated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It loses both cost-seekers and premium buyers to more committed rivals."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (11, 'Corporate strategy: diversification and vertical integration', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish business-level from corporate-level strategy, and reason about diversification and vertical integration using the ''better-off'' test. Warm-up: what question does corporate strategy answer that business strategy does not?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Business-level strategy asks how to win within a single industry. Corporate-level strategy asks which industries the firm should be in and how owning several businesses together creates more value than owning them separately. Diversification means entering new product markets. Related diversification enters markets that share resources or capabilities with the current business, so the parent can transfer skills or share costs; unrelated diversification enters markets with little in common, relying only on financial or management discipline, and it more often destroys value. The key screen is the better-off test: does combining these businesses under one roof make at least one of them genuinely stronger than it would be alone? Vertical integration is a special case - expanding along your own value chain. Backward (upstream) integration means owning your suppliers; forward (downstream) integration means owning your route to the customer. It can secure supply, protect quality, and capture margin, but it adds fixed costs and reduces flexibility."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A tech company that makes both the devices and the operating system practices vertical integration to control quality and the customer experience end to end. A streaming service that once only licensed shows moved upstream into producing its own originals - backward integration into content that reduces dependence on studios and differentiates the catalog. A consumer-goods conglomerate spanning detergents, snacks, and razors is diversified; the related brands share distribution and marketing muscle, which passes the better-off test, whereas a random grab-bag of unrelated businesses often fails it and gets broken up later. Owning the supply chain is not free: a firm that builds its own factories takes on heavy fixed costs and loses the option to switch suppliers when technology shifts."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which level of strategy asks ''which industries should we compete in''? 2) A shoe brand buying the tannery that supplies its leather is integrating in which direction? 3) What is the better-off test? 4) Why is unrelated diversification riskier than related diversification? 5) Name one cost or downside of vertical integration. Answers: 1) Corporate-level strategy. 2) Backward (upstream) - toward its supplier. 3) Whether combining the businesses makes at least one of them stronger than it would be standing alone. 4) It shares few resources or capabilities, so the parent adds little beyond financial oversight and often destroys value. 5) It raises fixed costs and reduces flexibility to switch suppliers or partners when conditions change."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming bigger and more diversified is automatically better - if the businesses do not make each other stronger, the combination fails the better-off test. Mistake: confusing vertical integration (moving along your own value chain) with diversification (entering a different product market). Exit: (a) What direction is forward integration? (b) What single test should any diversification move pass? Answers: forward (downstream) integration moves toward the customer/route to market; the better-off test - the combination must make at least one business genuinely stronger."}]}'::jsonb, 'Business strategy asks how to win in one arena; corporate strategy asks which arenas to be in at all. Different level, different question.', 13),
  (12, 'Growth and market entry: build, buy, partner, and going international', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Choose among the build, buy, and partner routes into a new market and weigh the trade-offs of the main modes of international entry. Warm-up: if speed matters most and you lack a capability, is building it yourself usually the fastest route?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Once a firm decides where to grow - a familiar tool is the grid of existing versus new products and existing versus new markets - it must choose how to get there. There are three routes. Build (organic): develop the capability or product in-house; cheapest in cash and best for control, but slowest and riskiest when you lack the know-how. Buy (acquire): purchase a company that already has it; fast and buys instant capability, but expensive, and many acquisitions fail on culture and integration. Partner (alliance, joint venture, license): share risk and combine strengths without full ownership; flexible, but you depend on a partner and may hand over knowledge. International entry adds its own ladder of commitment: exporting is low-cost and low-control; licensing or franchising lets a local party operate your model; a joint venture shares a local partner''s market knowledge; a wholly owned subsidiary gives the most control at the most risk and cost. More control generally means more commitment and more exposure."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A large software firm that wanted a professional-network product it lacked chose to buy rather than build, acquiring an established platform to gain the users and technology at once. A beverage company entering many countries franchises and partners with local bottlers, trading some control for speed and local knowledge across dozens of markets. A retailer testing a foreign market often begins by exporting or opening a joint venture before committing to wholly owned stores, climbing the commitment ladder only as confidence grows. When a fast-moving rival threatens and the clock matters more than the price, buy or partner usually beats build; when control and secrecy matter and time allows, building in-house wins."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three routes into a new capability or market. 2) Which route gives the fastest access to a capability you lack, and what is its main risk? 3) In international entry, which mode offers the most control but the most risk? 4) Why might a firm export before opening wholly owned operations abroad? 5) A firm needs a niche technology urgently and cannot secrecy-protect building it - which route fits? Answers: 1) Build (organic), buy (acquire), and partner (alliance or joint venture). 2) Buy - it grants instant capability, but integration and culture clashes make many acquisitions fail. 3) A wholly owned subsidiary. 4) To test demand with low cost and low commitment before risking heavy investment. 5) Buy or partner, since building would be too slow and offers no secrecy advantage here."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: defaulting to acquisition because it feels decisive, while underestimating integration risk. Mistake: choosing an international mode by cost alone and ignoring how much control you need. Exit: (a) What are the three growth routes? (b) In international entry, how do control and risk move together across the modes? Answers: build, buy, and partner; more control (toward a wholly owned subsidiary) generally comes with more commitment, cost, and risk, while low-control modes like exporting carry less of each."}]}'::jsonb, 'Build, buy, partner is the how of entry; the Ansoff grid (existing vs new products and markets) is the where. Answer where first, then choose how.', 14),
  (13, 'Business models and the Business Model Canvas', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what a business model is and use the nine blocks of the Business Model Canvas to describe how a firm actually makes money. Warm-up: name the three verbs at the heart of any business model."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A business model is the logic by which an organization creates, delivers, and captures value. The Business Model Canvas lays this out on nine connected blocks. At the center is the Value Proposition - the bundle that solves a customer''s problem. On the customer side sit Customer Segments (who you serve), Channels (how you reach them), and Customer Relationships (how you keep them). On the operations side sit Key Activities (what you must do), Key Resources (what you must have), and Key Partners (who helps you). Underneath run the two money blocks: the Revenue Streams (how value is captured - one-time sales, subscription, usage fees, advertising, licensing) and the Cost Structure (what it costs to run). The canvas is powerful because it is a system: change one block and others must move with it. Shifting from selling software to renting it, for example, changes revenue streams, relationships, and key activities all at once."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A razor maker famously runs a ''razor-and-blades'' model: sell the handle cheaply (a low-margin channel to acquire customers) and capture value through repeat blade sales - a revenue-stream choice that shapes the whole model. A software firm that moved from selling boxed licenses to a subscription changed its revenue stream to recurring fees, which in turn changed its customer relationship (ongoing service, not a one-time sale) and its key activities (continuous updates). A marketplace serves two customer segments at once - buyers and sellers - and captures value by taking a fee on transactions, with network effects as its key resource. Each is a different arrangement of the same nine blocks."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What three things does a business model do with value? 2) On the canvas, which block sits at the center? 3) Under which block would a subscription fee fall? 4) A firm switches from one-time sales to a rental model - name two blocks that must change besides Revenue Streams. 5) Why is the canvas described as a system rather than a checklist? Answers: 1) Creates, delivers, and captures value. 2) The Value Proposition. 3) Revenue Streams. 4) Customer Relationships (ongoing rather than one-off) and Key Activities (continuous service and updates); Cost Structure also shifts. 5) Because the blocks interlock - changing one forces changes in others, so it must stay internally consistent."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing the business model (the value-creating machine) with strategy (how you beat rivals) - related but not the same. Mistake: editing one canvas block in isolation and leaving the model internally inconsistent. Exit: (a) Name the nine blocks in any order. (b) Which two blocks capture the money side? Answers: Value Proposition, Customer Segments, Channels, Customer Relationships, Key Activities, Key Resources, Key Partners, Revenue Streams, Cost Structure; the money side is Revenue Streams and Cost Structure."}]}'::jsonb, 'Strategy is how you beat rivals; a business model is the machine that creates, delivers, and captures value. Two firms can share a strategy yet run different models, and vice versa.', 13),
  (14, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Corporate-level strategy is chiefly concerned with...","options":["how to beat rivals in one industry","which industries the firm should compete in","how to price a single product","which advertising channel to use"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Corporate strategy chooses the arenas; business strategy competes within one."},{"prompt":"A coffee retailer buying the farms that grow its beans is an example of...","options":["forward integration","backward integration","unrelated diversification","a joint venture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moving upstream toward suppliers is backward (upstream) integration."},{"prompt":"The fastest way to gain a capability you completely lack is usually to...","options":["build it organically","buy a company that has it","wait for it to appear","cut costs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Acquiring grants instant capability, though integration risk is high."},{"prompt":"Which international entry mode gives the most control but the most risk?","options":["Exporting","Licensing","Joint venture","Wholly owned subsidiary"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A wholly owned subsidiary maximizes control at the highest commitment and risk."},{"prompt":"On the Business Model Canvas, a monthly subscription fee is part of...","options":["Key Resources","Revenue Streams","Channels","Value Proposition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"How the firm captures money is the Revenue Streams block."},{"prompt":"The block at the heart of the Business Model Canvas is the...","options":["Cost Structure","Key Partners","Value Proposition","Customer Segments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Everything else is arranged around the Value Proposition."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (15, 'Execution: KPIs and the balanced scorecard', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why execution decides whether a strategy succeeds, tell a real KPI from a vanity metric, and lay out the four perspectives of the balanced scorecard. Warm-up: why can a brilliant strategy still fail?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A strategy only exists in action. Execution turns the chosen where and how into coordinated daily work - aligning structure, incentives, and measurement so people actually do the things the strategy requires. Measurement is the steering wheel. A key performance indicator (KPI) is a metric tied directly to a strategic objective, one the team can influence and that informs a decision. A vanity metric looks impressive but drives no action - total registered users flatters a report, while weekly active users or retention actually tells you if the strategy is working. The balanced scorecard was created because judging a firm on financial numbers alone is like driving by the rear-view mirror: money results report the past. It adds three forward-looking perspectives. Financial: are we delivering results to owners? Customer: how do customers see us? Internal Process: which activities must we excel at? Learning and Growth: can our people and systems keep improving? Together the four connect long-term capability to short-term results, and each objective gets its own KPI."},{"heading":"Illustrations","text":"A subscription business that lives or dies on retention tracks monthly churn and customer lifetime value as its core KPIs, not raw sign-ups, because those numbers move the decisions that matter. A manufacturer chasing a differentiation strategy watches defect rates and on-time delivery under the Internal Process perspective, knowing that quality is what its customers pay a premium for. Under Learning and Growth, a firm betting on innovation tracks the share of revenue from products launched in the last three years - a leading indicator that tomorrow''s financial results are being built today. The scorecard''s discipline is that every strategic objective must have a measure, so no priority hides from accountability."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why can a great strategy still fail? 2) Total registered users versus weekly active users - which is more likely a vanity metric? 3) Name the four balanced-scorecard perspectives. 4) Under which perspective would ''employee skills and system readiness'' fall? 5) Give the test that separates a KPI from a vanity metric. Answers: 1) Because execution - aligning structure, incentives, and daily action - can fail even when the plan is sound. 2) Total registered users, since it only grows and rarely drives a decision. 3) Financial, Customer, Internal Process, and Learning and Growth. 4) Learning and Growth. 5) A KPI is tied to a strategic objective, can be influenced by the team, and changes a decision; a vanity metric fails those tests."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: judging performance on financial results alone, which only report the past. Mistake: celebrating vanity metrics that rise no matter what and never inform a choice. Exit: (a) What problem was the balanced scorecard designed to solve? (b) What three properties make a metric a real KPI? Answers: over-reliance on backward-looking financial measures, by adding customer, process, and learning perspectives; a KPI is tied to a strategic objective, is influenceable by the team, and informs a decision."}]}'::jsonb, 'A good KPI is tied to a strategic choice and can be moved by the team. If a number cannot change a decision, it is a vanity metric, not a KPI.', 13),
  (16, 'Capstone: a full strategic analysis of a company', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Chain every framework in this course into one coherent strategic analysis of a real company, ending in a recommendation you can defend. Warm-up: in what order should the frameworks run, and why does order matter?"},{"heading":"The task","text":"Pick one company you know well and produce a complete analysis in five steps. Step 1, external: run a focused PESTEL to find the two or three macro forces that could reshape the industry, then a Five Forces read to judge how profitable the industry itself is and where the pressure sits. Step 2, internal: list the firm''s main resources and capabilities and run the strongest one or two through VRIO to see what could carry a lasting advantage. Step 3, synthesis: build a SWOT from steps 1 and 2, making sure strengths are VRIO-worthy and threats are real external forces, then cross the boxes into candidate moves. Step 4, choice: state the firm''s competitive advantage (cost leadership, differentiation, or a focus niche) and, at the corporate level, whether any diversification, vertical integration, or growth-and-entry move is warranted - and by which route (build, buy, or partner). Step 5, execution: define two or three KPIs, mapped to balanced-scorecard perspectives, that would tell you within a year whether the recommendation is working."},{"heading":"A worked mini-example","text":"Take a premium electric-vehicle maker. External: PESTEL flags Environmental and Legal forces (emissions rules, charging infrastructure) as tailwinds and Economic forces (interest rates, subsidy changes) as risks; Five Forces shows rising rivalry as legacy carmakers enter and real substitute pressure from cheaper models. Internal: battery and software capability plus a charging network run through VRIO as valuable, rare, and costly to imitate - a genuine advantage - while manufacturing scale is more easily copied. Synthesis: a SWOT crosses the battery strength against the threat of new entrants into a move - extend the lead in range and charging before rivals close the gap. Choice: differentiation at the premium end, with forward integration into the charging network defended on the better-off test; growth into new segments favors building on the existing platform over buying. Execution: track deliveries and gross margin (Financial), charging-network uptime and owner satisfaction (Customer), and battery cost per unit of range (Internal Process). Use this as a model of how the pieces connect - your job is to run the same chain on a company of your own choosing, in your own words."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what order do the frameworks run in this analysis? 2) Why should a SWOT strength be VRIO-worthy? 3) Which two decisions belong to the ''choice'' step? 4) Give one KPI you would map to the Customer perspective for the example firm. 5) Why does the analysis end with execution measures rather than the strategy statement? Answers: 1) PESTEL and Five Forces (external), then resources and VRIO (internal), then SWOT (synthesis), then the competitive and corporate choice, then execution KPIs. 2) Because a strength that is not rare and hard to copy gives no real advantage, so it should not anchor the strategy. 3) The competitive advantage (cost, differentiation, or focus) and any corporate move (diversification, vertical integration, or growth route). 4) Owner satisfaction or charging-network uptime as customers experience it. 5) Because a strategy exists only in action; without measures you cannot tell within a year whether it is working."},{"heading":"Self-check rubric","text":"Check yes to each: the external step names the few forces that could actually change the industry, not a long undifferentiated list; VRIO is applied to specific resources, not vague strengths; the SWOT is crossed into moves rather than left as four lists; the choice names a clear competitive advantage and, where relevant, a corporate move with a route (build, buy, or partner); and two or three KPIs are tied to balanced-scorecard perspectives and could show results within a year. If all five hold, you have produced a defensible strategic analysis rather than a description."}]}'::jsonb, 'Work outside-in then inside-out: environment first (PESTEL, Five Forces), then the firm (VRIO), then synthesis (SWOT), then the choice and how you will measure it.', 16),
  (17, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The two questions every strategy must answer are...","options":["how much and by when","where to compete and how to win","who to hire and how to pay them","what to build and where to sell it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strategy is choosing the arena (where) and the advantage (how to win)."},{"prompt":"Which is the clearest example of a vision rather than a mission?","options":["We move packages safely for small firms today","We are working toward a future of clean, universal transport","We repair before we replace","We serve short-haul commuters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A vision names a future state the firm is reaching toward."},{"prompt":"A carbon tax most naturally sits in which PESTEL lenses?","options":["Social and Technological","Environmental and Legal","Economic and Social","Technological and Political"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is an environmental measure enforced through law (with a political element)."},{"prompt":"In Porter''s model, a well-informed buyer who can switch sellers easily represents...","options":["low buyer power","high buyer power","a substitute","a barrier to entry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Easy switching and good information give buyers high bargaining power."},{"prompt":"A patent that blocks copycats most directly affects which force?","options":["Rivalry","Threat of new entrants","Supplier power","Threat of substitutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The patent is a barrier that lowers the threat of new entrants."},{"prompt":"In VRIO, a sustained competitive advantage requires that a resource be valuable, rare, costly to imitate, and...","options":["patented","organized to be exploited","cheap","owned by the founder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Organization test asks whether the firm can actually capture the value."},{"prompt":"A rival launching a competing product belongs in a SWOT under...","options":["Strengths","Weaknesses","Opportunities","Threats"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is an external development, so it is a Threat."},{"prompt":"A firm that is the lowest-cost producer but charges the market price is pursuing...","options":["differentiation","cost leadership","a focus niche","unrelated diversification"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lower cost at an equal price is cost leadership captured as margin."},{"prompt":"A shoe brand acquiring its leather supplier is practicing...","options":["forward integration","backward integration","related diversification","a licensing deal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moving upstream toward the supplier is backward integration."},{"prompt":"The ''better-off'' test asks whether...","options":["the firm is larger than rivals","combining businesses makes at least one genuinely stronger","profits rose last year","the CEO approves the deal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Diversification should make at least one business stronger than it would be alone."},{"prompt":"The international entry mode with the most control and the most risk is...","options":["exporting","licensing","a joint venture","a wholly owned subsidiary"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Full ownership maximizes control at the highest commitment and risk."},{"prompt":"On the Business Model Canvas, the block at the center is the...","options":["Cost Structure","Value Proposition","Key Partners","Channels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Value Proposition sits at the heart, with the other blocks arranged around it."},{"prompt":"The balanced scorecard was created mainly to counter...","options":["too many product launches","reliance on financial measures alone","weak mission statements","excessive partnering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It adds customer, process, and learning perspectives to backward-looking financials."},{"prompt":"Which is most clearly a vanity metric rather than a KPI?","options":["weekly active users","monthly churn rate","total registered users ever","customer lifetime value"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cumulative count that only rises and drives no decision is a classic vanity metric."},{"prompt":"''Share of revenue from products launched in the last three years'' fits which balanced-scorecard perspective?","options":["Financial","Customer","Internal Process","Learning and Growth"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is a leading indicator of future capability - Learning and Growth."},{"prompt":"In the recommended capstone order, the analysis runs from...","options":["execution to environment","internal to external only","external scan to internal to synthesis to choice to execution","SWOT to PESTEL to VRIO"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Work outside-in then inside-out, ending in the choice and its KPIs."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0003-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c' and l.position = v.pos
);
